course: dictionary
lesson: 12093
title: Coaxing: gentle persuading
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To coax someone is to gently persuade them to do something, using kindness and patience instead of force. «Mia used a soft, coaxing voice to get the kitten out from under the bed.» The kitten was not dragged out. It was gently talked out.
explain: Coaxing can be a noun, an adjective or a verb. «It took a lot of coaxing before Sam agreed to come swimming.» Here coaxing is a noun meaning the gentle persuading itself. Coax, coaxes, coaxed and coaxing all come from the same word.
explain: You can also coax things, not only people. «Dad knelt by the stove, coaxing the fire to light with small dry twigs.» Nobody is being persuaded here. It means handling something carefully and gently until it works.
fact: Coax means to gently persuade someone by being kind, patient and pleasant.
fact: «Coaxing» can be an adjective, as in «a coaxing voice», which means gentle and persuading.
fact: «With a bit of coaxing» means after some gentle persuading.
fact: You can coax someone into doing something or out of doing something, as in «coax the shy puppy out of its basket».
fact: Coaxing is gentle. Ordering, pushing or forcing someone is not coaxing.
exercise: «Mia used a soft, coaxing voice to get the kitten out.» What does coaxing mean here? | Running fast | Sleeping quietly | Loud and angry shouting | *Gentle persuading
exercise: «It took a lot of coaxing before Sam agreed to come swimming.» What is coaxing in this sentence? | A kind of swimming stroke | Ordering someone to go | *Kind, patient persuading over time | Ignoring someone completely
exercise: «Dad coaxed the fire to light with dry twigs.» What does coaxed mean? | Put out | *Gently got it to work by careful handling | Shouted at | Burned down
test: Which word means gently persuading someone by being kind and patient? | Shouting | *Coaxing | Ignoring | Forcing
test: Which phrase means after some gentle persuading? | *with a bit of coaxing | in a great hurry | with a bit of shouting | without a sound
exam: Which sentence uses coaxing correctly? | The rain coaxed the storm to start | *The mum spent ten minutes coaxing her son out of bed with kind words | The dog barked loudly at the postman | He coaxing the door shut angrily
